  "content": {
    "id": "p24-challenge-q4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "Triangle $ABC$ is equilateral with vertices $A(0,a)$, $B(m,0)$ and $C(-m,0)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [
      {
        "id": "p24-challenge-q4-diagram",
        "role": "question",
        "format": "tikz",
        "code": "% mathsmap-diagram-colours {\"version\":1,\"kind\":\"graph\",\"base\":[],\"semantic\":[],\"reference\":\"Source PDF page 24, printed page 342, Challenge 4\"}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1.2cm,y=1.2cm]\n\\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes=\"1\"/>}\n\\coordinate (A) at (0,{sqrt(3)});\n\\coordinate (B) at (1,0);\n\\coordinate (C) at (-1,0);\n\\draw[black,line width=0.5pt,->] (-2,0)--(2.3,0) node[right] {$x$};\n\\draw[black,line width=0.5pt,->] (0,-1.2)--(0,2.5) node[above] {$y$};\n\\draw[black,line width=0.8pt] (0,{sqrt(3)/3}) circle[radius={2*sqrt(3)/3}];\n\\draw[black,line width=0.8pt] (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle;\n\\fill[black] (A) circle[radius=1.5pt];\n\\fill[black] (B) circle[radius=1.5pt];\n\\fill[black] (C) circle[radius=1.5pt];\n\\node[above right] at (A) {$A(0,a)$};\n\\node[below right] at (B) {$B(m,0)$};\n\\node[below left] at (C) {$C(-m,0)$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}",
        "src": null,
        "widthMm": 70,
        "alt": "Mathematical diagram",
        "overlayOf": null,
        "transparent": false,
        "axes": null,
        "spec": {
          "sourcePage": 24,
          "sourceRegion": "Challenge exercise question 4, lower-right diagram",
          "description": "Equilateral triangle with horizontal base on the x-axis, upper vertex on the positive y-axis and its circumcircle extending below the x-axis. X is not marked. Source blue outlines and red vertex dots become ordinary black marks under house style; the origin O label is omitted under the graph convention.",
          "mathematics": "Representative coordinates use m=1 and a=sqrt(3); the circumcentre is (0,sqrt(3)/3) and radius is 2sqrt(3)/3. These construction coordinates are not printed numerical givens.",
          "answerVisibility": "Supplied figure remains visible in every edition; no perpendicular bisectors or X are supplied."
        },
        "derived": false,
        "reviewStatus": "needs-review"
      }
    ],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p24-challenge-q4-initial-proof",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "First show $a=\\sqrt{3}m$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a=\\sqrt{3}m$.",
          "worked": "Since the triangle is equilateral, $AB=BC=2m$. By Pythagoras,\n$$\\begin{align*}a^2+m^2&=(2m)^2\\\\a^2&=3m^2\\\\a&=\\sqrt{3}m.\\end{align*}$$\nThe positive root is used because $A$ is above the $x$-axis and $B$ is on its positive side.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 29
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-challenge-q4-a",
        "type": "group",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find, in terms of $a$, the equation of the perpendicular bisector of:",
        "layout": "grid",
        "columns": 2,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [
          {
            "id": "p24-challenge-q4-a-i",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "i",
            "prompt": "$AC$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$y=-\\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\dfrac{a}{3}$.",
              "worked": "The midpoint of $AC$ is $(-\\frac{m}{2},\\frac{a}{2})$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Gradient of }AC&=\\frac{a-0}{0-(-m)}\\\\\\text{Gradient of }AC&=\\sqrt{3}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe perpendicular gradient is $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$. Using the midpoint,\n$$\\begin{align*}y-\\frac a2&=-\\frac1{\\sqrt{3}}\\left(x+\\frac m2\\right)\\\\y&=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac a2-\\frac a6\\\\y&=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac a3.\\end{align*}$$",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 42
          },
          {
            "id": "p24-challenge-q4-a-ii",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "ii",
            "prompt": "$AB$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$y=\\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\dfrac{a}{3}$.",
              "worked": "The midpoint of $AB$ is $(\\frac{m}{2},\\frac{a}{2})$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Gradient of }AB&=\\frac{0-a}{m-0}\\\\\\text{Gradient of }AB&=-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{align*}$$\nThe perpendicular gradient is $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$. Using the midpoint,\n$$\\begin{align*}y-\\frac a2&=\\frac1{\\sqrt{3}}\\left(x-\\frac m2\\right)\\\\y&=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac a2-\\frac a6\\\\y&=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac a3.\\end{align*}$$",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 42
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-challenge-q4-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Show that the two perpendicular bisectors meet at $X\\left(0,\\dfrac a3\\right)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$X=\\left(0,\\dfrac a3\\right)$.",
          "worked": "At the intersection, the two expressions for $y$ are equal:\n$$\\begin{align*}-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac a3&=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\frac a3\\\\\\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{3}}&=0\\\\x&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nSubstitution into either equation gives $y=\\frac{a}{3}$, so the intersection is $X(0,\\frac{a}{3})$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-challenge-q4-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Find the distance $AX$ in terms of $a$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$AX=\\dfrac{2a}{3}$.",
          "worked": "Both points lie on the $y$-axis, with $A$ above $X$:\n$$\\begin{align*}AX&=a-\\frac a3\\\\AX&=\\frac{2a}{3}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 14
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-challenge-q4-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "Find the equation of the circle with centre $X\\left(0,\\dfrac a3\\right)$ and radius $AX$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x^2+\\left(y-\\dfrac a3\\right)^2=\\dfrac{4a^2}{9}$.",
          "worked": "Use $(x-h)^2+(y-k)^2=r^2$ with centre $(0,\\frac{a}{3})$ and radius $\\frac{2a}{3}$:\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+\\left(y-\\frac a3\\right)^2&=\\left(\\frac{2a}{3}\\right)^2\\\\x^2+\\left(y-\\frac a3\\right)^2&=\\frac{4a^2}{9}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      }
    ],
    "diagramPlacement": "beside-prompt"
  },
